<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Hayek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich August von Hayek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Hayek was an Austrian economist and philosopher of the Austrian school of economics, known for his contributions to political economy, political philosophy and intellectual history. He earned doctorates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>, became a British national in 1938, and taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and Freiburg. His most popular work, The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> (1944), has been republished many times, and his account of how prices communicate information led to his share of the 1974 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences with Gunnar Myrdal. He rejected the label conservative, preferring classical liberal or libertarian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek.jpg" alt="Friedrich Hayek" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was DickClarkMises at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Hayek_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Friedrich August von Hayek was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 8 May 1899 to August von Hayek, a medical doctor and part-time botany lecturer at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>, and Felicitas Hayek, née von Juraschek. In his teens Hayek fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and he said later that this experience, together with a wish to help avoid the mistakes that led to the war, drew him into economics. At the University of Vienna he first studied philosophy, psychology and economics, and he earned doctorates in law in 1921 and in political science in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With the help of Ludwig von Mises, Hayek directed the Austrian Institute for Business Cycle Research in the late 1920s. He joined the London School of Economics in 1931 at the invitation of Lionel Robbins. In Prices and Production (1931) he argued that the business cycle resulted from a central bank's credit expansion, which, by holding interest rates artificially low, caused capital to be misallocated. Building on Mises, he argued that central planners could never have enough information to allocate resources reliably.</p><p>The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> (1944) grew from his concern at the view in British academia that fascism was a capitalist reaction to socialism; Hayek argued that fascism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> and state socialism shared roots in central economic planning. In 1950 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>'s Committee on Social Thought, and from 1962 until 1968 he taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-freiburg">University of Freiburg</a>, where he worked on Law, Legislation and Liberty. On 9 October 1974 it was announced that he would share the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economics with Gunnar Myrdal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hayek also wrote on psychology. The Sensory Order (1952) set out a learning and memory model close to what became known as Hebbian learning. In social and political philosophy he based his views on the limits of human knowledge and on spontaneous order, favouring a market order in which the state mostly enforces abstract legal rules. In 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a>, newly elected leader of the Conservative Party, held up his book The Constitution of Liberty at a meeting and said that this was what the party believed. He received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1991 and died on 23 March 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrich Hayek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Hayek</b> and Milton Friedman held that central planning by an unchecked state bred inefficiency and stagnation, and the Hungarian economist János Kornai analysed the chronic shortages of the planned economies.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Hayek</b> argued in The Road to Serfdom (1944) that the market is the only way to decide what to produce and how to distribute it without coercion, and Milton Friedman that centralised economies are always accompanied by political repression.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Right-libertarianism, based on the works of Locke, Frédéric Bastiat, <b>Friedrich Hayek</b> and Ludwig von Mises, values institutions that support capitalist conditions.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>At the 1938 Colloquium that named neoliberalism, Ludwig von Mises denounced the ordoliberalism of the rival faction as “ordo-interventionism”, while Alexander Rüstow wrote that <b>Hayek</b> and Mises were relics of the liberalism that caused the Great Depression.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/neoliberalism">Neoliberalism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Friedrich Hayek"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4231 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4231 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
